+## Tutorial production plan — approved replacement, 2026-09-08
 
-Keep the ten lesson identities and the separate `SPEC-LSN-011` assessment. Break compound lessons into one-action substeps using `SPEC-TUT-005`; this avoids turning each lesson into an overloaded checklist. The required cycle remains explain → highlight/demonstrate → player action → authoritative proof → acknowledgement → operational reason → next step.
+Owner-approved implementation sequence: bottom console and shared chapter framework, complete Chapter 1, then individually qualified Chapters 2–7, then specializations. Tutorial access is optional and strongly recommended. Remove mandatory camera exercises. Historical ten-lesson and first-run gating findings above describe the superseded design; preserve their evidence, not their active sequence. SPEC-TUT-008 is the current contract.
 
-The readiness setting can reuse the M01 region, but it requires an explicit training deployment and event state, isolated from campaign consequences. Activate and explain only the actors needed by the current substep. The resolved M01 force has six Surveyors/two Lancers, while active `REL-FTU-007.AUTH` requires tutorial selection and movement of **three** Meridian Lancers. The present shared two-Lancer staging cannot satisfy that gate. Recommended reconciliation is a training-only third Lancer with its role explained; alternatively, Angelis may amend the tutorial count. Neither option changes the resolved M01 force automatically. Record the decision before freezing training deployment. Idle reserve personnel are visibly held for deployment rather than arbitrary interactive clutter. Do not invent extra geography or make the lesson’s practice Well overwrite M01’s founding doctrine.
+### Shared map and screen
 
-| Lesson and purpose | What must be present | What the human does and proves | Automatic behavior and recovery | Exit and later use |
-|---|---|---|---|---|
-| **1. Survey — inspect a route before committing a force.** `SPEC-LSN-001`, `REL-FTU-006`. | Anchor, readable Archive Recovery and Evacuation sites, minimap, camera controls; unrelated orders blocked. | Identify own Core; pan, zoom, recenter, and inspect the three required waypoints. Preserve the current 200 cm/30-tick waypoint contract pending any approved change. | Resolve active bindings; show the actual destination and acknowledgement; count player-driven camera observations only. Programmatic camera moves and stationary elapsed time cannot award the action. Pause/focus interruption preserves the substep. | The player can find home and the operation’s destination. Reused in every mission. |
-| **2. Roster — know which actor receives an order.** `SPEC-LSN-002`. | One clearly marked Surveyor near the Anchor and its actual selection/command panel. | Select the Surveyor; inspect purpose, health, order, and available actions; deselect on clear terrain. Explain the difference between selection and command. | Publish the correct selected-unit details; wrong selection gives a specific correction; a tooltip alone does not prove understanding. | Selection evidence plus a human comprehension check of the Surveyor’s job. Reused for all named witnesses/carriers. |
-| **3. Section muster — organize the right force.** `SPEC-LSN-003`, `REL-FTU-007`. | Reconciled training roster, distinct worker/combat silhouettes, group/subgroup feedback; resolve the two-versus-three-Lancer conflict above. | Box-select; modify and restore selection; cycle subgroup; assign and recall a control group. Teach these as separate substeps. Include selection of three Lancers and their required arrival at the extraction beacon across this lesson/Route until the active requirement is amended. | Maintain group membership and highlight the active subgroup; recover an accidental empty group without resetting the whole tutorial. | Player can recall the intended section and avoid sending workers into combat. Verify all three arrive within the existing 400 cm gate. Reused for divided defense from M03 onward. |
-| **4. Route check — issue, revise, and cancel a meaningful order.** `SPEC-LSN-004`. | Named route destination, Matter seam, patrol route, Bulwark/Surveyor guard pair, and one clearly invalid target. | Move and arrive; issue a context order; stop an active order; patrol; guard; intentionally inspect a rejected order as a safety check. | Execute movement/pathfinding, show current order and route, and explain refusal. Teach the reason for the rejection instead of asking for arbitrary failure. | Actual accepted-order effects and rejection acknowledgement. Later supports escort, avoidance, and response to route closure. |
-| **5. Reserve — fund the next useful action.** `SPEC-LSN-005`, `REL-FTU-008`. | Matter node, Surveyor, valid drop-off, delivery route, reserve monitor, and the upcoming construction/production cost. | Assign gathering, observe cargo become credited Matter, inspect throughput, and meet the existing 200 delivered-Matter requirement. Explain what this pays for. | Continue gather/deliver after assignment; distinguish carried resources from credited resources. Show blocked/full/idle cases. Preserve exact behavior at depletion; do not mask a broken route with free Matter. | Earned delivery and an intelligible resource monitor. Reused for every economic commitment. |
-| **6. Link restoration — make infrastructure operational.** `SPEC-LSN-006`, `REL-FTU-009`. | Named corridor work site, valid/invalid placement previews, appropriate worker tools, powered/unpowered comparison, assist opportunity, and repair target. Existing [training staging](../Content/World/Source/Authoring/training_readiness_staging_v1.json) provides link/repair sites. | Inspect placement validity; commit construction; assist; repair; verify operational/network state. Reconcile the script’s Power Link lesson with `REL-FTU-009`’s Foundry/Aegis connection coverage rather than dropping either. | Execute assigned construction/repair and update power visuals. Incorrect placement does not charge resources. Interrupted work offers resume/reassign. | The player explains why the corridor now works. Reused in M03, M05, M06, M09–M13. |
-| **7. Foundry — prepare the next force.** `SPEC-LSN-007`, `REL-FTU-010`. | Completed production structure, clear costs/prerequisites, queue, Logistics meter, safe rally, and a coming perimeter requirement. | Queue a useful Lancer; inspect reserved capacity; cancel a queued item and see the refund rule; set rally; receive a genuinely produced unit there. | Process queue and rally movement; show missing prerequisites or capacity. Do not count a staged reinforcement as production proof. | Player can maintain reinforcements while directing a force. Reused in M03/M09 and the readiness fight. |
-| **8. Perimeter probe — preserve a working force under controlled pressure.** `SPEC-LSN-008`. | Authored small hostile probe with known entry, friendly line/screen/support roles, readable cover and a retreat area. | Attack-move, focus a suitable target, use stance/cover, protect the worker, and retreat a threatened unit. Teach each before requiring simultaneous control. | Spawn/activate only the announced drill threat; execute combat normally; explain losses and offer a phase retry. No attack escalation while the player is reading instruction. | Probe resolved while satisfying the authored survival condition. Reused in route defense and witness protection. |
-| **9. The board — find and prioritize what is happening elsewhere.** `SPEC-LSN-009`. | An actual current objective, completed-objective history, a spatial alert, minimap, and distinguishable unseen/visible/last-known areas. | Locate the affected site through the alert/minimap; read the objective change; scout to refresh a last-known contact; distinguish stale information from a visible target. | Publish truthful player-scoped observations and accessible alerts; suppress irrelevant notices. UI focus must not issue world orders. | Player can find the next action without an external explanation. Reused particularly in M06–M13. |
